{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_0","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"id\":\"R-2:8\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:109\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Both passages set human action within a wait for a divine event: pardon lasts until God brings His command, while the barrier lasts until the Lord’s promise arrives and it is leveled.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:109\",\"ar\":\"فَٱعْفُوا۟ وَٱصْفَحُوا۟ حَتَّىٰ يَأْتِىَ ٱللَّهُ بِأَمْرِهِۦٓ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:98\",\"ar\":\"فَإِذَا جَآءَ وَعْدُ رَبِّى جَعَلَهُۥ دَكَّآءَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s completed barrier is explicitly given an endpoint in the immediately following ayah, 18:98.\",\"limits\":\"The passages do not identify the command in 2:109 with the promise in 18:98; the parallel is their temporal framing.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:127\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The barrier joins the Quran’s scenes of communal building: Abraham and Ishmael raise the House’s foundations together, while Dhu al-Qarnayn asks people to help build a defensive rampart.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:127\",\"ar\":\"وَإِذْ يَرْفَعُ إِبْرَٰهِۦمُ ٱلْقَوَاعِدَ مِنَ ٱلْبَيْتِ وَإِسْمَٰعِيلُ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:95\",\"ar\":\"فَأَعِينُونِى بِقُوَّةٍ أَجْعَلْ بَيْنَكُمْ وَبَيْنَهُمْ رَدْمًا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"ءَاتُونِى زُبَرَ ٱلْحَدِيدِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus follows Dhu al-Qarnayn’s request for help with a construction project and specifies the materials and labor.\",\"limits\":\"The House is sacred and raised by two named builders; the rampart is a communal defense project with a different purpose.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:187\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:229\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:230\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-2:250\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The prayer أَفْرِغْ عَلَيْنَا صَبْرًا uses the focus’s same verb-and-preposition frame, but asks God to pour patience onto people; Dhu al-Qarnayn orders a literal pour of metal onto the barrier.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"2:250\",\"ar\":\"قَالُوا۟ رَبَّنَآ أَفْرِغْ عَلَيْنَا صَبْرًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ ءَاتُونِىٓ أُفْرِغْ عَلَيْهِ قِطْرًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus supplies a concrete object, قطر, for a pouring expression also used with the abstract صبر.\",\"limits\":\"The battle prayer is addressed to God and seeks steadfastness; the focus is a human instruction about metallurgical work.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:126\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-3:184\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"زُبَرَ الحديد shares its root with الزُّبُر in a list of revealed writings, opening a possible material-and-scriptural wordplay around the iron pieces.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"زُبَرَ ٱلْحَدِيدِ\"},{\"ref\":\"3:184\",\"ar\":\"بِٱلْبَيِّنَٰتِ وَٱلزُّبُرِ وَٱلْكِتَٰبِ ٱلْمُنِيرِ\"}],\"missing\":\"The verses establish the shared root but do not say the iron is inscribed or that زُبَر here carries a writing sense; a dictionary linking these senses or an inscription cue in the barrier scene could establish the wordplay.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:5\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:9\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The barrier called سَدًّا in Dhu al-Qarnayn’s request shares a root with the instruction to speak قولًا سديدًا, in a passage concerned with protecting vulnerable descendants; this could connect physical safeguarding with sound speech.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:94\",\"ar\":\"أَن تَجْعَلَ بَيْنَنَا وَبَيْنَهُمْ سَدًّۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"4:9\",\"ar\":\"وَلْيَقُولُوا۟ قَوْلًۭا سَدِيدًا\"}],\"missing\":\"The passages do not make the connection explicit; a lexicon showing whether سديد retains an image of closing or firmness, or a stronger contextual link between the speech and the wall, could decide whether this root echo matters.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:13\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:14\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:78\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The focus’s rampart blocks attackers for a time, while 4:78 says even fortified towers cannot prevent death; 18:98 then states that the barrier itself will be leveled at the Lord’s promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"4:78\",\"ar\":\"وَلَوْ كُنتُمْ فِى بُرُوجٍۢ مُّشَيَّدَةٍۢ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:97\",\"ar\":\"فَمَا ٱسْطَٰعُوٓا۟ أَن يَظْهَرُوهُ وَمَا ٱسْتَطَٰعُوا۟ لَهُۥ نَقْبًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:98\",\"ar\":\"فَإِذَا جَآءَ وَعْدُ رَبِّى جَعَلَهُۥ دَكَّآءَ\"}],\"activation\":\"The surrounding account shows both the barrier’s success against its immediate attackers and its limit at the appointed divine event.\",\"limits\":\"The verses do not name one another; 4:78 speaks of death reaching people in towers, not of Gog and Magog.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-4:163\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The singular زَبُورًا granted to David confirms a Quranic scriptural use of the z-b-r root that may resonate with the iron زُبَر in the focus.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"زُبَرَ ٱلْحَدِيدِ\"},{\"ref\":\"4:163\",\"ar\":\"وَءَاتَيْنَا دَاوُۥدَ زَبُورًۭا\"}],\"missing\":\"Nothing in the focus says the iron pieces bear writing; a dictionary linking the material and scriptural senses, or evidence of inscriptions in the barrier episode, could show whether this root echo changes the image.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-5:48\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:9\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"Both passages use جَعَلَ with an object pronoun and an accusative state complement: 6:9 imagines making an angel a man, while the focus makes the structure نارًا.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"6:9\",\"ar\":\"وَلَوْ جَعَلْنَٰهُ مَلَكًۭا لَّجَعَلْنَٰهُ رَجُلًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا جَعَلَهُۥ نَارًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s resultative construction provides the grammatical point of contact.\",\"limits\":\"The scenes and agents differ; this syntactic echo does not make the heating a transformation like the imagined change in 6:9.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"low\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:46\",\"status\":\"open\",\"finding\":\"The rare noun ٱلصَّدَفَيْنِ shares the ṣ-d-f root with the verbs for turning away from signs in 6:46 and 6:157; a root-level relation could place the wall’s paired sides beside Quranic language of turning away.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"بَيْنَ ٱلصَّدَفَيْنِ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:46\",\"ar\":\"ثُمَّ هُمْ يَصْدِفُونَ\"},{\"ref\":\"6:157\",\"ar\":\"وَصَدَفَ عَنْهَا\"}],\"missing\":\"The verses establish the root match but not a semantic bridge between the topographic noun and turning away; a dictionary tracing that relationship could determine whether it is active here.\",\"support\":\"weak\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:107\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-6:157\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"same-as:R-6:46\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:38\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:46\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The Quran’s other scene of a boundary between two realms describes a حِجَابٌ across which people can call to the Garden; the focus’s engineered rampart instead cannot be climbed or bored through.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"7:46\",\"ar\":\"وَبَيْنَهُمَا حِجَابٌۭ\"},{\"ref\":\"7:46\",\"ar\":\"وَنَادَوْا۟ أَصْحَٰبَ ٱلْجَنَّةِ أَن سَلَٰمٌ عَلَيْكُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:97\",\"ar\":\"فَمَا ٱسْطَٰعُوٓا۟ أَن يَظْهَرُوهُ وَمَا ٱسْتَطَٰعُوا۟ لَهُۥ نَقْبًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"Both scenes put a separating structure between groups, while the focus’s following ayah specifies how physically impassable its barrier is.\",\"limits\":\"The verses use different terms and describe different settings; the people calling across the حجاب are not the communities in the Dhu al-Qarnayn account.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:57\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:74\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"Thamud carve houses from mountains, while Dhu al-Qarnayn uses the space between opposing faces to build a rampart; the passages present two distinct ways people reshape mountain terrain.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"7:74\",\"ar\":\"وَتَنْحِتُونَ ٱلْجِبَالَ بُيُوتًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"سَاوَىٰ بَيْنَ ٱلصَّدَفَيْنِ\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s work takes place between the paired cliff faces, making the mountain setting of the Thamud passage a material parallel.\",\"limits\":\"One passage describes dwellings cut into mountains and the other a defensive barrier across a pass; the text does not connect their peoples or purposes.\",\"support\":\"medium\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:104\",\"status\":\"none\",\"code\":\"no-link\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:126\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The threatened believers’ prayer أَفْرِغْ عَلَيْنَا صَبْرًا repeats the focus’s pour-upon frame: there, patience is requested for people facing reprisals; here, molten metal is poured onto a structure meant to protect a community.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"7:126\",\"ar\":\"رَبَّنَآ أَفْرِغْ عَلَيْنَا صَبْرًۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"7:127\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ سَنُقَتِّلُ أَبْنَآءَهُمْ وَنَسْتَحْىِۦ نِسَآءَهُمْ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ ءَاتُونِىٓ أُفْرِغْ عَلَيْهِ قِطْرًا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus’s literal pouring of metal gives concrete form to the same verb and preposition used in the prayer for patience.\",\"limits\":\"The prayers ask God to grant an inward quality; Dhu al-Qarnayn directs a physical operation, and the passages do not identify the speakers.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"R-7:143\",\"status\":\"reading\",\"finding\":\"The barrier’s later fate echoes the mountain at Sinai: both are described with جَعَلَهُۥ دَكًّا, placing Dhu al-Qarnayn’s successful construction within a Quranic pattern of terrain leveled by divine action.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"7:143\",\"ar\":\"فَلَمَّا تَجَلَّىٰ رَبُّهُۥ لِلْجَبَلِ جَعَلَهُۥ دَكًّۭا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:98\",\"ar\":\"فَإِذَا جَآءَ وَعْدُ رَبِّى جَعَلَهُۥ دَكَّآءَ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا جَعَلَهُۥ نَارًۭا\"}],\"activation\":\"The immediate continuation of the focus says the barrier will be leveled when the Lord’s promise comes, using nearly the same result phrase as 7:143.\",\"limits\":\"At Sinai the mountain is leveled after the Lord’s manifestation; 18:98 speaks of the barrier at the promised event and does not identify the scenes.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"high\"}\n{\"id\":\"X1\",\"status\":\"note\",\"finding\":\"The travel narratives shift a supply request from food to construction material: Moses asks for their meal at 18:62, and Dhu al-Qarnayn twice orders material brought in 18:96.\",\"evidence\":[{\"ref\":\"18:62\",\"ar\":\"ءَاتِنَا غَدَآءَنَا\"},{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"ءَاتُونِى زُبَرَ ٱلْحَدِيدِ\"},{\"ref\":\"18:96\",\"ar\":\"قَالَ ءَاتُونِىٓ أُفْرِغْ عَلَيْهِ قِطْرًا\"}],\"activation\":\"The focus is the material-gathering phase of the next travel narrative, after Dhu al-Qarnayn reaches the people between the barriers.\",\"limits\":\"The repeated root is common and the passage does not mark an explicit allusion; the supplies serve different immediate needs.\",\"support\":\"strong\",\"relevance\":\"medium\"}"}}
